  "text": "2013B06 Briefly describe the\nbreakdown of haemoglobin after red cell lysis.\nList:\n·\nHb structure\n·\nIn the RES\n·\nIn the blood\n·\nIn the liver\n·\nEnterohepatic circulation and\nexcretion\nHaemoglobin (Hb)\nstructure:\n·\nComplex protein in RBCs\n·\n4 x haem (protoporphyrin with central\nFe\n2+\n)\n·\n4 x globin (polypeptide, mostly\nα\n2\nβ\n2\nin adult)\nIn\nthe reticulo-endothelial system (RES):\n·\ni.e. spleen red pulp, lymph nodes\n·\nRBC destroyed after ~ 120 days\n·\nIron transported via ferritin to the bone marrow\nfor re-incorporation\n·\nGlobin degraded into amino acids for\nrecycling\n·\nProtoporphyrin converted into bilirubin and\nexcreted\nIn\nthe blood:\n·\nBilirubin is unconjugated,\nlipid-soluble\n·\nDiffuses into blood\n·\nBound to albumin\nIn\nthe liver:\n·\nBR taken up by hepatocyte\n·\nBR-DG (water-soluble) secreted into bile (NOTE:\nrate limiting step), enters intestine\nEnterohepatic circulation and\nexcretion:\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
